{"name":"Filtered Light","hex":"#b1b2c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"filtered","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["With a filter (e.g., a cigarette).","Having been filtered (e.g., coffee grounds)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of filter"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/filtered"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Filtration","blocks":[{"text":"Filtration is a physical separation process that separates solid matter and fluid from a mixture using a filter medium that has a complex structure through which only the fluid can pass. Solid particles that cannot pass through the filter medium are described as oversize and the fluid that passes through is called the filtrate. Oversize particles may form a filter cake on top of the filter and may also block the filter lattice, preventing the fluid phase from crossing the filter, known as blinding. The size of the largest particles that can successfully pass through a filter is called the effective pore size of that filter. The separation of solid and fluid is imperfect; solids will be contaminated with some fluid and filtrate will contain fine particles. Filtration occurs both in nature and in engineered systems; there are biological, geological, and industrial forms. In everyday usage the verb \"strain\" is more often used; for example, using a colander to drain cooking water from cooked pasta."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/FilterDiagram.svg/330px-FilterDiagram.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filtration"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Hot_Filtration_set_up.jpg/500px-Hot_Filtration_set_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Hot_Filtration_set_up.jpg/500px-Hot_Filtration_set_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hot filtration, solution contained in the Erlenmeyer flask is heated on a hot plate to prevent re-crystallization of solids in the flask itself","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHot_Filtration_set_up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Hot_FIltration.jpg/500px-Hot_FIltration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Hot_FIltration.jpg/500px-Hot_FIltration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hot filtration for the separation of solids from a hot solution","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHot_FIltration.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cold_Filtration.jpg/500px-Cold_Filtration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cold_Filtration.jpg/500px-Cold_Filtration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cold filtration, the ice bath is used to cool down the temperature of the solution before undergoing the filtration process","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACold_Filtration.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/FilterFunnelApparatus.png/330px-FilterFunnelApparatus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/FilterFunnelApparatus.png/330px-FilterFunnelApparatus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Filter flask (suction flask, with sintered glass filter containing sample). Note the almost colourless filtrate in the receiver flask.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFilterFunnelApparatus.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG/500px-Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG/1280px-Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG/500px-Small_stationary_Bauer_HP_compressor_installation_DSC09403.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Small stationary Bauer HP breathing air compr